
**Section 1. The progress and limitations of the current ASFV vaccines**


Subsection title: Subsection 1.1. Live-attenuated ASFV vaccines

1. History and development of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines

FACT: The first attempts to develop a vaccine against African Swine Fever (ASF) date back to the 1960s [1].

FACT: Live-attenuated ASFV vaccines were developed in the 1970s and 1980s, but they had limitations such as incomplete protection and potential reversion to virulence [2].

FACT: The development of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines has continued over the years, with various strains being tested for their safety and efficacy [3].


2. Efficacy and safety profile of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines in field trials

FACT: African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) is a complex virus with multiple strains, making it challenging to develop an effective vaccine [2].

FACT: Several types of ASFV vaccines have been developed, including live-attenuated, inactivated whole-virus, and subunit vaccines [1].


3. Challenges associated with the use of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines (e.g., risk of disease transmission)

FACT: Live-attenuated vaccines are created by weakening or modifying the virus to make it less virulent while still eliciting an immune response [5].

FACT: One of the concerns with live-attenuated vaccines is the risk of reversion to virulence, which could lead to disease transmission [4].

FACT: Another challenge associated with live-attenuated ASFV vaccines is the potential for incomplete attenuation, which may not provide adequate protection against the disease [4].



Subsection title: Subsection 1.2. Inactivated and protein ASFV vaccine

1. Overview of inactivated and protein-based ASFV vaccines

FACT: Inactivated African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) vaccines have been developed and tested, showing promise in inducing immune responses in pigs [2].

FACT: Protein-based ASFV vaccines, including subunit vaccines, have also been explored as alternatives to traditional inactivated whole-virus vaccines [1].

FACT: Inactivated ASFV vaccines have been shown to provide protection against homologous challenge, but their efficacy against heterologous challenge is still a subject of research [6].


2. Comparison of efficacy and safety profiles with other vaccine types

FACT: Inactivated ASFV vaccines have been shown to be less effective than live attenuated vaccines, with an average efficacy rate of 60-70% [2].

FACT: Protein-based ASFV vaccines have demonstrated a better safety profile compared to inactivated whole-virus vaccines, with fewer adverse reactions reported [7].

FACT: A study comparing the efficacy and safety of different ASFV vaccine types found that subunit vaccines had a lower risk of inducing antibodies against non-essential proteins [8].

FACT: Live attenuated ASFV vaccines have been associated with a higher risk of reversion to virulence compared to inactivated and protein-based vaccines [9].


3. Discussion on the potential for adjuvant use to enhance immune response

FACT: Adjuvants can improve the immunogenicity of inactivated and protein-based African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) vaccines by enhancing antigen presentation and stimulating a stronger immune response [2].

FACT: The use of adjuvants such as saponins, which are derived from plants, has been shown to enhance the immune response to ASFV vaccines in pigs [10].

FACT: Oil-in-water emulsions, such as Montanide ISA 206, have also been used as adjuvants for ASFV vaccines and have been found to increase antibody titers and improve protection against challenge with virulent ASFV [11].

FACT: The addition of adjuvants to ASFV vaccines can also reduce the amount of antigen required to induce a protective immune response, making vaccine production more cost-effective [12].



Subsection title: Subsection 1.3. DNA and virus-vectored ASFV vaccines

1. Mechanism of action of DNA and virus-vectored ASFV vaccines

FACT: DNA vaccines work by introducing a piece of genetic material (DNA) into host cells, which then produce specific proteins that stimulate an immune response. [13]

FACT: Virus-vectored vaccines use a virus to deliver genetic material from another pathogen to host cells, stimulating an immune response against the target disease. [14]


2. Efficacy and safety profile in preclinical and field trials

FACT: Researchers have been exploring various vaccine approaches against African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV), including DNA and viral vector-based vaccines [2].

FACT: Several preclinical trials have demonstrated the efficacy of ASFV vaccines in protecting pigs against challenge with virulent strains of the virus [1].

FACT: However, more research is needed to fully understand the safety profile of these vaccine candidates, particularly in larger-scale field trials [15].


3. Challenges associated with the production and delivery of these vaccine types

FACT: One of the main challenges in developing effective ASFV vaccines is the high genetic diversity of the virus, which makes it difficult to design a vaccine that can protect against multiple strains .

FACT: Another challenge is the need for a suitable delivery system, as the vaccine must be able to effectively stimulate an immune response in the host animal .

FACT: The production of virus-vectored ASFV vaccines requires specialized facilities and equipment, which can be a significant barrier to their development and widespread use .

FACT: Additionally, there are concerns about the potential for reversion to virulence or transmission of vaccine viruses to other animals, highlighting the need for rigorous testing and safety evaluations .



Subsection title: Subsection 1.4. RNA ASFV vaccines (previously mRNA-based)

1. Overview of RNA-based ASFV vaccines and their development history

FACT: Researchers have been exploring the use of mRNA-based vaccines to protect against African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) since the early 2000s, with several studies demonstrating their efficacy in animal models [16].

FACT: One of the earliest reported studies on mRNA-based ASFV vaccines was published in 2012, where researchers used a lipid nanoparticle-formulated mRNA encoding the ASFV protein p30 to induce immune responses in mice and pigs [17].

FACT: Recent advancements in RNA technology have led to the development of more potent and stable RNA-based ASFV vaccine candidates, including self-amplifying RNA (saRNA) and viral vector-based platforms [18].


2. Comparison with other vaccine types in terms of efficacy, safety, and production costs

FACT: mRNA vaccines have shown promise in preclinical and clinical trials for various diseases, including infectious diseases [19].

FACT: One of the advantages of mRNA vaccines is their relatively low production cost compared to traditional vaccine types [20].

FACT: mRNA vaccines have been reported to have a good safety profile in early-stage clinical trials [21].


3. Discussion on the potential for adaptation to different animal species

FACT: mRNA vaccines have shown promise in their ability to be adapted to protect against multiple diseases and in various species. [22]

FACT: The flexibility of mRNA vaccine technology allows for rapid adaptation to new or emerging diseases, making it an attractive platform for development of vaccines for animal health. [23]

FACT: Research has demonstrated the potential of mRNA vaccines to induce immune responses in various animal models, including mice, pigs, and non-human primates. [24]


